SAT_CHUNK("rw/cross-text/3/1",[
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.b3a6c5cba1","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-passage_data2-21","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some researchers argue that allowing students to choose which books they read in school increases how much they read. When a group of middle schools replaced assigned novels with a large classroom library, students could pick any book as long as they wrote a short reflection afterward.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>A two-year study of middle schools found that students given free choice of books did read more pages overall. However, the same students were less likely to attempt books above their current reading level, and their scores on tests of challenging vocabulary grew more slowly than those of students who also read some assigned texts.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By arguing that reflections are more important than the books students read","By claiming that students given choice actually read less than students given assigned books","By suggesting that assigned novels should be removed from all middle schools","By agreeing that choice may increase reading but cautioning that it can come with drawbacks for building advanced skills"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Text 2 confirms students read more pages but notes they avoided harder books and gained challenging vocabulary more slowly. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.49c94bf396","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-passage_data2-21","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some researchers argue that allowing students to choose which books they read in school increases how much they read. When a group of middle schools replaced assigned novels with a large classroom library, students could pick any book as long as they wrote a short reflection afterward.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>A two-year study of middle schools found that students given free choice of books did read more pages overall. However, the same students were less likely to attempt books above their current reading level, and their scores on tests of challenging vocabulary grew more slowly than those of students who also read some assigned texts.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes a difference in how the authors of Text 1 and Text 2 view student choice in reading?","choices":["Text 1 presents it as a benefit, while Text 2 suggests that its benefits may be accompanied by costs.","Text 1 focuses on high school students, while Text 2 focuses on college students.","Text 1 relies on test scores, while Text 2 relies on students' reflections.","Text 1 argues that it lowers reading volume, while Text 2 argues that it raises reading volume."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Text 1 emphasizes increased reading; Text 2 adds evidence of drawbacks. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.6defd891d4","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-passage_data2-24","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some nutrition writers claim that eating late at night inevitably leads to weight gain, because the body supposedly burns fewer calories while sleeping.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Controlled studies that give participants the same total calories at different times of day have generally found little difference in weight change. Late-night eating may be linked to weight gain in surveys, but that link often reflects what people eat at night, such as high-calorie snacks, and how much they eat overall, not the timing itself.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, the surveys linking late-night eating to weight gain may be misleading because","choices":["the people who eat at night may also choose higher-calorie foods or eat more overall.","controlled studies found that nighttime eating increases metabolism.","participants in the surveys ate only in the morning.","the surveys measured weight only once."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Text 2 says the link \"often reflects what people eat at night ... and how much they eat overall.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.035f7f0591","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-passage_data2-25","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Critics sometimes dismiss graphic novels as simplified versions of \"real\" literature, suitable mainly for readers who struggle with traditional books.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Reading a graphic novel well requires skills that prose alone does not develop. Readers must interpret how images and words interact, notice details in the artwork that contradict or complicate the text, and infer what happens in the gaps between panels. Far from being a simplified form, the graphic novel asks readers to combine two ways of making meaning at once.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 mentions \"the gaps between panels\" primarily to","choices":["explain why graphic novels are shorter than prose novels.","describe a printing error common in graphic novels.","give an example of something readers must interpret on their own.","criticize artists who leave out important scenes."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Inferring what happens in the gaps is one of the skills the author lists. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.ff57f309e2","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-passage_data2-26","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Advocates of four-day school weeks argue that the schedule saves districts money on transportation and utilities and gives teachers more time to plan.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>In several rural districts that adopted four-day weeks, savings turned out to be smaller than expected, typically a few percent of the budget. Some families also reported difficulty finding child care on the fifth day. Although teachers generally liked the schedule, results for student achievement were mixed across districts.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, the authors would most likely agree that four-day school weeks","choices":["cost districts more than five-day weeks.","have been adopted mainly in large cities.","are generally appealing to teachers.","lead to higher test scores in every district."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Text 1 says they give teachers planning time; Text 2 says teachers generally liked the schedule. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.782505cd61","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-passage_data2-27","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Many birdwatchers believe that feeding wild birds in winter helps them survive the cold months by providing extra energy when natural food is scarce.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Winter feeding can help some birds survive, but it can also have unintended effects. In one region, feeders encouraged certain species to stay farther north instead of migrating, and crowds at feeders helped spread diseases among birds. Researchers suggest keeping feeders clean and spacing them apart to reduce these risks.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By claiming that feeding birds always causes them to die","By suggesting that feeders should be placed close together","By agreeing that feeding can help birds survive while warning that it can also cause problems that should be managed","By arguing that birds never need extra food in winter"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Text 2 grants that feeding \"can help\" but notes changed migration and disease, and recommends precautions. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.428564afcf","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-304","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some nutritionists recommend that people eat many small meals throughout the day instead of three larger ones, arguing that frequent eating keeps metabolism running faster and helps with weight control.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Metabolism does rise slightly after every meal as the body digests food, but the total increase depends on how much is eaten, not how many times. In controlled studies where participants ate the same total calories in either three meals or six smaller ones, their total energy use and weight change were essentially the same.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely agree with Text 1's claim that","choices":["metabolism increases after eating.","meal timing matters more than total calories.","small frequent meals lead to greater weight loss.","three meals a day slow metabolism permanently."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Text 2 acknowledges \"metabolism does rise slightly after every meal.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.d4174b653d","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-304","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some nutritionists recommend that people eat many small meals throughout the day instead of three larger ones, arguing that frequent eating keeps metabolism running faster and helps with weight control.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Metabolism does rise slightly after every meal as the body digests food, but the total increase depends on how much is eaten, not how many times. In controlled studies where participants ate the same total calories in either three meals or six smaller ones, their total energy use and weight change were essentially the same.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes how the author of Text 2 responds to the reasoning in Text 1?","choices":["The author accepts part of the reasoning but rejects the conclusion drawn from it.","The author accepts the conclusion but offers a different reason.","The author ignores the reasoning and discusses a different topic.","The author rejects every part of the reasoning."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Text 2 grants the metabolic rise but denies that meal frequency changes the total. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.27d76cb70c","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-305","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Historians have often portrayed a certain nineteenth-century inventor as a lone genius who developed the electric light bulb through sheer persistence, testing thousands of materials by himself.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Laboratory records show that the inventor worked with a large team of machinists, chemists, and assistants who carried out many of the experiments. Moreover, several earlier inventors had already produced working bulbs, though none lasted long. The inventor's achievement, some historians now argue, was organizing collaborative research and improving existing designs, not working alone.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By agreeing that the inventor tested every material by himself","By claiming that the inventor had no role in developing the light bulb","By arguing that the inventor relied heavily on a team and built on earlier inventors' work rather than working alone","By suggesting that earlier bulbs lasted longer than the inventor's"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Text 2 cites laboratory records of a team and earlier working bulbs. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.04f4b4c7d0","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-305","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Historians have often portrayed a certain nineteenth-century inventor as a lone genius who developed the electric light bulb through sheer persistence, testing thousands of materials by himself.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Laboratory records show that the inventor worked with a large team of machinists, chemists, and assistants who carried out many of the experiments. Moreover, several earlier inventors had already produced working bulbs, though none lasted long. The inventor's achievement, some historians now argue, was organizing collaborative research and improving existing designs, not working alone.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, both authors would most likely agree that the inventor","choices":["invented the first light bulb ever made.","worked without any assistants.","played a role in the development of a practical light bulb.","failed to improve on earlier designs."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Text 1 credits him; Text 2 credits his improvements and organization. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.1868e15112","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-306","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Supporters of homework-free policies in elementary schools argue that homework adds stress without improving learning, and that children benefit more from play and family time after school.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Research on elementary homework shows mixed results. Short, well-designed assignments, such as reading with a parent for fifteen minutes, have been linked to gains in reading. Long worksheets that repeat class material show little benefit and can cause frustration. The question, according to many researchers, is not whether to assign homework but what kind to assign.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By claiming that long worksheets are the most effective homework","By arguing that play has no benefit for children","By agreeing that all homework should be eliminated","By suggesting that the value of homework depends on the type of assignment rather than rejecting all homework"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Text 2 says short reading assignments help, while repetitive worksheets do not. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.db78249d46","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-306","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Supporters of homework-free policies in elementary schools argue that homework adds stress without improving learning, and that children benefit more from play and family time after school.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Research on elementary homework shows mixed results. Short, well-designed assignments, such as reading with a parent for fifteen minutes, have been linked to gains in reading. Long worksheets that repeat class material show little benefit and can cause frustration. The question, according to many researchers, is not whether to assign homework but what kind to assign.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely agree with the supporters in Text 1 that","choices":["family time should be replaced by homework.","play is harmful to children.","all homework should be banned.","some homework can cause frustration without improving learning."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Text 2 says long worksheets \"can cause frustration.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.bd1c0a7f76","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-307","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Wildlife managers in some regions have argued that removing predators such as wolves will increase deer populations and therefore improve hunting opportunities.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>In one region where wolves were removed, deer numbers did rise, but within a decade the deer had overgrazed young trees and shrubs. As food became scarce, deer grew thinner, disease spread, and populations crashed. Ecologists concluded that predators can help keep prey populations within limits that their habitat can support.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By agreeing that removing predators permanently improves deer populations","By claiming that wolves cause deer to overgraze","By suggesting that deer populations never change","By arguing that removing predators may increase deer at first but can lead to overgrazing and population crashes"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Text 2 describes an initial rise followed by overgrazing and collapse. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.393d2b7567","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-307","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Wildlife managers in some regions have argued that removing predators such as wolves will increase deer populations and therefore improve hunting opportunities.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>In one region where wolves were removed, deer numbers did rise, but within a decade the deer had overgrazed young trees and shrubs. As food became scarce, deer grew thinner, disease spread, and populations crashed. Ecologists concluded that predators can help keep prey populations within limits that their habitat can support.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, the deer population crashed mainly because","choices":["hunters removed too many deer.","wolves returned to the region.","deer ate so much vegetation that food became scarce.","the climate became warmer."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Overgrazing led to scarce food, thinner deer, and disease. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.bcf00b8b79","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-307","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Wildlife managers in some regions have argued that removing predators such as wolves will increase deer populations and therefore improve hunting opportunities.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>In one region where wolves were removed, deer numbers did rise, but within a decade the deer had overgrazed young trees and shrubs. As food became scarce, deer grew thinner, disease spread, and populations crashed. Ecologists concluded that predators can help keep prey populations within limits that their habitat can support.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the relationship between the two texts?","choices":["Text 2 supports the reasoning in Text 1 with additional evidence.","Text 2 discusses predators in an unrelated habitat.","Text 2 summarizes Text 1.","Text 2 describes long-term consequences that challenge the reasoning in Text 1."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Text 1 expects benefits; Text 2 shows eventual harm. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.00189cb557","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-308","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some critics argue that audiobooks are a lesser form of reading, claiming that listeners absorb less of a book's meaning than people who read print.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>In one study, participants who listened to a short story and participants who read it scored similarly on questions about plot and character. Listeners actually recalled more details about characters' emotions, which the researchers attributed to narrators' vocal expression. The researchers noted, however, that readers found it easier to reread difficult passages.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, listeners recalled characters' emotions better most likely because","choices":["listeners took notes while listening.","listeners reread difficult passages.","narrators conveyed emotion through their voices.","the story was shorter for listeners."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The researchers \"attributed\" it to \"narrators' vocal expression.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.c1ddf629f0","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-309","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Many parents believe that playing classical music for infants makes them smarter, an idea popularized decades ago by reports of a so-called music effect.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>The original study behind the claim tested college students, not infants, and found only a small, temporary improvement on one spatial reasoning task after listening to a sonata. Later attempts to repeat the result often failed. No research has shown that simply playing classical music for babies raises their intelligence.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By pointing out that the evidence behind the belief is weak and does not concern infants","By claiming that music harms infants' development","By agreeing that classical music raises infants' intelligence","By arguing that college students are smarter than infants"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Text 2 notes the study involved adults, showed a small temporary effect, and often failed to replicate. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.bfd83c036e","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-309","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Many parents believe that playing classical music for infants makes them smarter, an idea popularized decades ago by reports of a so-called music effect.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>The original study behind the claim tested college students, not infants, and found only a small, temporary improvement on one spatial reasoning task after listening to a sonata. Later attempts to repeat the result often failed. No research has shown that simply playing classical music for babies raises their intelligence.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, which statement about the original study is accurate?","choices":["It tested hundreds of infants over several years.","It found that sonatas lower test scores.","It proved a permanent increase in intelligence.","It found a small, temporary effect on one task in college students."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Text 2 describes a \"small, temporary improvement on one spatial reasoning task.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.1650275f34","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-311","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some economists argue that raising taxes on sugary drinks will reduce consumption, because higher prices discourage buyers.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>After one city added a tax on sugary drinks, sales within the city fell sharply. However, sales at stores just outside the city limits rose, as some shoppers crossed the border to buy cheaper drinks. Overall consumption among residents dropped, but by less than the in-city sales figures alone suggested.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By claiming that the tax increased consumption among residents","By suggesting that the city should remove the tax immediately","By agreeing that the tax reduced consumption while noting that the effect was smaller than in-city sales suggested","By arguing that prices have no effect on buyers"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Text 2 shows reduced consumption, partly offset by cross-border shopping. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.83e17371a6","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-312","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Many people assume that the Middle Ages were a period in which scientific thinking came to a halt in Europe.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Historians of science point out that medieval scholars developed new theories of motion, studied optics, and built mechanical clocks. Universities founded during the period became centers for debating natural philosophy. While some ancient texts were lost in Europe, others were preserved, translated, and expanded upon, often through contact with scholars in the Islamic world.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, contact with scholars in the Islamic world contributed to","choices":["the preservation and expansion of ancient texts.","the loss of all ancient knowledge.","the closing of European universities.","the invention of the printing press."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Texts were \"preserved, translated, and expanded upon\" through that contact. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.0b4f8f0d71","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-312","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Many people assume that the Middle Ages were a period in which scientific thinking came to a halt in Europe.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Historians of science point out that medieval scholars developed new theories of motion, studied optics, and built mechanical clocks. Universities founded during the period became centers for debating natural philosophy. While some ancient texts were lost in Europe, others were preserved, translated, and expanded upon, often through contact with scholars in the Islamic world.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 acknowledges that","choices":["some ancient texts were lost in Europe during the period.","medieval clocks did not work.","universities discouraged debate.","no scientific progress occurred."],"answer":"A","explanation":"\"While some ancient texts were lost in Europe...\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.409c827730","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-313","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Proponents of year-round schooling argue that the long summer break causes students to forget much of what they learned, forcing teachers to spend weeks reviewing each fall.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Studies of year-round calendars, which spread the same number of school days across shorter breaks, have found only small differences in overall achievement compared with traditional calendars. Some evidence suggests that summer learning loss is largest among students from low-income families, who may have fewer educational opportunities during the break.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, a year-round calendar differs from a traditional calendar mainly in","choices":["the subjects that are taught.","how school days are distributed across the year.","the total number of school days.","the age at which students begin school."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It \"spread[s] the same number of school days across shorter breaks.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.4a002d3d67","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-313","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Proponents of year-round schooling argue that the long summer break causes students to forget much of what they learned, forcing teachers to spend weeks reviewing each fall.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Studies of year-round calendars, which spread the same number of school days across shorter breaks, have found only small differences in overall achievement compared with traditional calendars. Some evidence suggests that summer learning loss is largest among students from low-income families, who may have fewer educational opportunities during the break.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely suggest that efforts to reduce summer learning loss","choices":["should begin in high school.","are unnecessary for all students.","should eliminate summer entirely.","could focus especially on students with fewer opportunities during the break."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Loss is \"largest among students from low-income families.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.991c56451b","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-315","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Museum curators have traditionally argued that fragile artifacts should never be handled by visitors, since even careful touching can cause gradual damage.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Some museums now use high-quality replicas, created from detailed scans, that visitors are encouraged to touch. Surveys suggest that handling replicas helps visitors understand an object's weight and texture, and the originals remain safely behind glass. Curators who once opposed hands-on displays have found that replicas address their main concern.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By suggesting that museums should remove all displays","By arguing that visitors should handle all original artifacts","By claiming that touching artifacts never causes damage","By agreeing that originals should be protected while describing a way to let visitors handle objects without risking damage"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Replicas let people touch while originals stay protected. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.6286141f09","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-315","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Museum curators have traditionally argued that fragile artifacts should never be handled by visitors, since even careful touching can cause gradual damage.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Some museums now use high-quality replicas, created from detailed scans, that visitors are encouraged to touch. Surveys suggest that handling replicas helps visitors understand an object's weight and texture, and the originals remain safely behind glass. Curators who once opposed hands-on displays have found that replicas address their main concern.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, handling replicas benefits visitors by helping them","choices":["avoid visiting museums.","repair damaged artifacts.","understand objects' weight and texture.","identify which objects are fake."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Surveys suggest replicas help with \"weight and texture.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.191e03a93c","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-316","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some urban planners argue that building more highway lanes is the best way to relieve traffic congestion.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Studies of many cities have found that adding highway capacity often leads to more driving. When roads become less crowded, people take trips they previously avoided, choose to live farther away, or shift from transit to cars. Within a few years, traffic frequently returns to its previous levels, a pattern known as induced demand.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes how Text 2 responds to Text 1?","choices":["It supports the proposed solution with new data.","It proposes adding even more lanes than Text 1 suggests.","It agrees but adds that roads should be tolled.","It presents evidence suggesting that the proposed solution may not work in the long run."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Traffic \"returns to its previous levels.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.daa1e715df","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-317","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Many writers claim that people have become worse at paying attention because of smartphones, citing a widely repeated claim that human attention spans are now shorter than a goldfish's.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Researchers who have searched for the source of the goldfish comparison have been unable to find any study supporting it. Attention also varies greatly by task; people who struggle to focus on a dull lecture may concentrate for hours on a game or a novel. Whether smartphones affect attention is a legitimate question, but the goldfish claim is not evidence.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By arguing that the goldfish comparison is unsupported, even though the question about smartphones deserves study","By suggesting that people cannot concentrate on novels","By claiming that smartphones improve attention","By agreeing that attention spans are shorter than a goldfish's"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Text 2 rejects the statistic but keeps the question open. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.fc9fce6f44","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-317","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Many writers claim that people have become worse at paying attention because of smartphones, citing a widely repeated claim that human attention spans are now shorter than a goldfish's.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Researchers who have searched for the source of the goldfish comparison have been unable to find any study supporting it. Attention also varies greatly by task; people who struggle to focus on a dull lecture may concentrate for hours on a game or a novel. Whether smartphones affect attention is a legitimate question, but the goldfish claim is not evidence.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely agree with Text 1 that","choices":["attention spans are shorter than a goldfish's.","the goldfish statistic comes from a reliable study.","people can no longer read novels.","the effect of smartphones on attention is worth investigating."],"answer":"D","explanation":"\"Whether smartphones affect attention is a legitimate question.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.b49b7d6578","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-318","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some architects argue that tall glass skyscrapers are energy-efficient because they let in abundant daylight, reducing the need for electric lighting.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Glass walls do admit daylight, but they also let heat escape in winter and absorb heat in summer, often requiring powerful heating and cooling systems. Studies of office towers have found that fully glazed buildings frequently use more total energy than buildings with a mix of windows and insulated walls, even after lighting savings are counted.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By agreeing that glass skyscrapers use less total energy than other buildings","By claiming that glass blocks daylight","By suggesting that buildings should have no windows","By arguing that lighting savings are often outweighed by increased heating and cooling needs"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Heat loss and gain can exceed lighting savings. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.dcfc64bfc3","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-318","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some architects argue that tall glass skyscrapers are energy-efficient because they let in abundant daylight, reducing the need for electric lighting.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Glass walls do admit daylight, but they also let heat escape in winter and absorb heat in summer, often requiring powerful heating and cooling systems. Studies of office towers have found that fully glazed buildings frequently use more total energy than buildings with a mix of windows and insulated walls, even after lighting savings are counted.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely agree with the architects in Text 1 that glass walls","choices":["let in daylight.","prevent heat from escaping.","keep buildings cool in summer.","reduce total energy use."],"answer":"A","explanation":"\"Glass walls do admit daylight.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.7af099fbf6","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-320","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some historians have argued that a small ancient kingdom collapsed suddenly because of a single devastating invasion.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Excavations at the kingdom's cities reveal a more gradual process. Layers of soil show declining harvests over several decades, and records mention repeated droughts and rising grain prices. The invasion occurred, but it struck a kingdom already weakened by years of hardship.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By agreeing that the invasion alone caused the collapse","By arguing that the kingdom was already weakened by long-term problems before the invasion occurred","By claiming that no invasion ever took place","By suggesting that the kingdom grew stronger before the invasion"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Text 2 describes gradual decline plus the invasion. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.dd7032c5be","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-320","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some historians have argued that a small ancient kingdom collapsed suddenly because of a single devastating invasion.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Excavations at the kingdom's cities reveal a more gradual process. Layers of soil show declining harvests over several decades, and records mention repeated droughts and rising grain prices. The invasion occurred, but it struck a kingdom already weakened by years of hardship.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely agree with the historians in Text 1 that","choices":["grain prices fell.","the collapse happened in a single day.","an invasion occurred.","droughts were unimportant."],"answer":"C","explanation":"\"The invasion occurred.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.98b179c9a6","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-321","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some linguists predict that the spread of global languages such as English will cause many local languages to disappear within the next century.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Language loss is a serious concern, and many small languages are indeed threatened. Yet some communities have revived languages that had few remaining speakers by creating immersion schools, recording elders, and using the languages in media and apps. These efforts show that decline is not inevitable when communities commit resources to preservation.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By arguing that English will soon disappear","By agreeing that many languages are threatened while arguing that community efforts can reverse decline","By suggesting that recording elders speeds language loss","By claiming that no languages are threatened"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Text 2 accepts the threat but describes successful revivals. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.3f6a561504","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-321","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some linguists predict that the spread of global languages such as English will cause many local languages to disappear within the next century.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Language loss is a serious concern, and many small languages are indeed threatened. Yet some communities have revived languages that had few remaining speakers by creating immersion schools, recording elders, and using the languages in media and apps. These efforts show that decline is not inevitable when communities commit resources to preservation.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, which approach has helped revive threatened languages?","choices":["Teaching only written grammar","Avoiding the use of apps","Creating immersion schools","Banning the use of global languages"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Immersion schools are one listed method. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.2b511af916","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-321","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some linguists predict that the spread of global languages such as English will cause many local languages to disappear within the next century.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Language loss is a serious concern, and many small languages are indeed threatened. Yet some communities have revived languages that had few remaining speakers by creating immersion schools, recording elders, and using the languages in media and apps. These efforts show that decline is not inevitable when communities commit resources to preservation.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes a difference between the texts?","choices":["Text 1 focuses on English, while Text 2 focuses on mathematics.","Text 1 presents language loss as likely, while Text 2 emphasizes that loss can sometimes be prevented.","Text 1 relies on community examples, while Text 2 relies on predictions.","Text 1 argues languages are growing, while Text 2 argues they are shrinking."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Prediction versus evidence of revival. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.9c2dbf0500","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-322","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Many people believe that lightning never strikes the same place twice.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Tall structures and high points in a landscape are struck repeatedly. Some skyscrapers are hit dozens of times each year, and lightning rods are designed precisely because tall objects attract strikes again and again. The saying may be meant as encouragement, but it is not accurate.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 mentions skyscrapers primarily to","choices":["give an example that contradicts the belief in Text 1.","describe the history of lightning rods.","explain how lightning forms.","argue that skyscrapers are unsafe."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Skyscrapers are struck \"dozens of times each year.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.a6bd1cd9b0","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-322","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Many people believe that lightning never strikes the same place twice.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Tall structures and high points in a landscape are struck repeatedly. Some skyscrapers are hit dozens of times each year, and lightning rods are designed precisely because tall objects attract strikes again and again. The saying may be meant as encouragement, but it is not accurate.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, the saying about lightning is best described as","choices":["inaccurate, though possibly meant as encouragement.","a rule for designing lightning rods.","scientifically proven.","a description of skyscrapers."],"answer":"A","explanation":"\"May be meant as encouragement, but it is not accurate.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.afcf4ab3f3","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-323","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Advocates of organic farming argue that it is better for the environment in every respect than conventional farming.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Organic farms often support greater biodiversity and use fewer synthetic pesticides. However, they typically produce smaller harvests per acre, which means more land may be needed to grow the same amount of food. Whether organic farming is better overall can depend on how much land is available and which environmental impacts are measured.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, a drawback of organic farming is that it","choices":["uses more synthetic pesticides.","may require more land to produce the same amount of food.","reduces biodiversity.","cannot grow vegetables."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Smaller harvests per acre mean more land may be needed. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.ab37c019bb","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-324","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some teachers believe that students should memorize multiplication tables, arguing that quick recall frees attention for more complex math.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Other educators worry that memorization without understanding leaves students unable to apply facts flexibly. They favor teaching strategies such as breaking problems into parts, so that students understand why facts are true. Many researchers conclude that the approaches work best together: understanding makes facts meaningful, and fluent recall makes complex work easier.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By suggesting that memorization is most useful when combined with understanding","By agreeing that understanding is unnecessary","By claiming that memorization harms all students","By arguing that multiplication should not be taught"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Text 2 concludes the two approaches work best together. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.c653af7d86","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-324","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some teachers believe that students should memorize multiplication tables, arguing that quick recall frees attention for more complex math.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Other educators worry that memorization without understanding leaves students unable to apply facts flexibly. They favor teaching strategies such as breaking problems into parts, so that students understand why facts are true. Many researchers conclude that the approaches work best together: understanding makes facts meaningful, and fluent recall makes complex work easier.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, the educators who worry about memorization are concerned that students","choices":["may not be able to use facts flexibly without understanding them.","will memorize too quickly.","cannot break problems into parts.","will refuse to learn multiplication."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Memorization without understanding limits application. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.6d5f39e7dc","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-324","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some teachers believe that students should memorize multiplication tables, arguing that quick recall frees attention for more complex math.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Other educators worry that memorization without understanding leaves students unable to apply facts flexibly. They favor teaching strategies such as breaking problems into parts, so that students understand why facts are true. Many researchers conclude that the approaches work best together: understanding makes facts meaningful, and fluent recall makes complex work easier.</p>","prompt":"The researchers described in Text 2 would most likely agree with the teachers in Text 1 that","choices":["multiplication tables are too difficult.","fluent recall of facts can make complex work easier.","strategies should replace memorization.","understanding facts is unimportant."],"answer":"B","explanation":"\"Fluent recall makes complex work easier.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.272246db4b","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-325","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>A popular idea holds that people are either \"left-brained,\" meaning logical and analytical, or \"right-brained,\" meaning creative and intuitive.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Brain imaging studies show that both hemispheres are active during nearly all complex tasks, including math and art. Some functions, such as aspects of language, rely more on one side, but researchers have found no evidence that individuals consistently use one hemisphere more than the other.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By arguing that art uses no brain activity","By agreeing that people are either left-brained or right-brained","By claiming that the brain has only one hemisphere","By presenting evidence that people do not rely predominantly on one side of the brain"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Imaging shows both hemispheres active; no evidence of dominance. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.ff7bb83121","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-326","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some critics argue that video games are a waste of time with no educational value.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Research on certain action games has found that players can improve in tracking multiple moving objects and switching attention quickly. Puzzle and strategy games may encourage planning and problem solving. The benefits depend heavily on the type of game, and researchers caution that many popular games have not been shown to teach specific skills.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By agreeing that all video games are a waste of time","By arguing that attention skills cannot be improved","By claiming that every video game teaches valuable skills","By noting that some types of games may develop certain skills, while cautioning that benefits vary by game"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Text 2 finds benefits for some games but not all. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.9ade738759","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-327","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some city officials argue that planting trees along streets is mainly a way to make neighborhoods more attractive.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Street trees provide measurable benefits beyond appearance. Studies have linked tree-lined streets to cooler summer temperatures, reduced stormwater runoff, and in some neighborhoods, lower energy costs for nearby homes. Researchers suggest that trees should be treated as public infrastructure, like sidewalks and drainage systems.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By agreeing that trees serve only a decorative purpose","By suggesting that sidewalks are unnecessary","By arguing that street trees offer practical benefits beyond making neighborhoods attractive","By claiming that trees increase energy costs"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Cooler streets, less runoff, lower energy costs. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.97cdde2578","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-327","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some city officials argue that planting trees along streets is mainly a way to make neighborhoods more attractive.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Street trees provide measurable benefits beyond appearance. Studies have linked tree-lined streets to cooler summer temperatures, reduced stormwater runoff, and in some neighborhoods, lower energy costs for nearby homes. Researchers suggest that trees should be treated as public infrastructure, like sidewalks and drainage systems.</p>","prompt":"The researchers in Text 2 compare trees to sidewalks and drainage systems mainly to","choices":["describe how trees are planted.","explain why sidewalks crack.","suggest that trees provide essential public services.","argue that trees should be removed."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Treating trees \"as public infrastructure.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.64f1aa337e","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-327","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some city officials argue that planting trees along streets is mainly a way to make neighborhoods more attractive.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Street trees provide measurable benefits beyond appearance. Studies have linked tree-lined streets to cooler summer temperatures, reduced stormwater runoff, and in some neighborhoods, lower energy costs for nearby homes. Researchers suggest that trees should be treated as public infrastructure, like sidewalks and drainage systems.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, both authors would most likely agree that street trees","choices":["can make neighborhoods more attractive.","increase stormwater runoff.","raise summer temperatures.","have no measurable effects."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Text 2 says benefits go \"beyond appearance,\" implying appearance is one benefit. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.7d13645323","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-328","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Many people believe that bats are blind.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>All bat species can see, and some large fruit bats have excellent vision suited to finding food at dusk. Many smaller bats rely heavily on echolocation, emitting sounds and listening to echoes to navigate and hunt in darkness, which may be why people assume bats cannot see.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By claiming that no bats use echolocation","By agreeing that most bats are blind","By arguing that bats hunt only during the day","By stating that bats can see and suggesting that reliance on echolocation may explain the mistaken belief"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Text 2 says all bats can see and explains the misconception. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.d069bfa145","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-328","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Many people believe that bats are blind.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>All bat species can see, and some large fruit bats have excellent vision suited to finding food at dusk. Many smaller bats rely heavily on echolocation, emitting sounds and listening to echoes to navigate and hunt in darkness, which may be why people assume bats cannot see.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes how Text 2 responds to the belief in Text 1?","choices":["It agrees but adds that bats cannot hear.","It corrects the belief and offers a possible reason people hold it.","It supports the belief with new evidence.","It ignores the belief."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Correction plus explanation via echolocation. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.5de9215db1","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-329","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some business leaders argue that open-plan offices, without walls or private offices, encourage collaboration by making it easy for employees to talk.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>A study that tracked employees before and after two companies switched to open-plan offices found that face-to-face interaction actually decreased by about seventy percent, while email and messaging increased. Researchers suggest that employees, lacking privacy, may have used headphones and digital messages to avoid being overheard or interrupted.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By agreeing that open offices greatly increase conversation","By presenting evidence that open-plan offices may reduce face-to-face interaction rather than increase it","By arguing that private offices prevent all collaboration","By claiming that email decreased in open offices"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Face-to-face interaction dropped sharply. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.6b81d8df2e","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-329","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some business leaders argue that open-plan offices, without walls or private offices, encourage collaboration by making it easy for employees to talk.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>A study that tracked employees before and after two companies switched to open-plan offices found that face-to-face interaction actually decreased by about seventy percent, while email and messaging increased. Researchers suggest that employees, lacking privacy, may have used headphones and digital messages to avoid being overheard or interrupted.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, employees in open-plan offices most likely increased their use of email and messaging because they","choices":["wanted to avoid being overheard or interrupted.","were required to stop talking.","could not use headphones.","had more private offices."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Lack of privacy led to digital communication. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.dea5e337a0","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-330","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some conservation groups argue that zoos mainly serve entertainment and do little for wild animal populations.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Many accredited zoos run breeding programs for endangered species, and several species that had vanished from the wild have been reintroduced from zoo-bred populations. Zoos also fund field research and habitat protection. Critics are right that not all zoos meet high standards, but well-run zoos can make meaningful contributions to conservation.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, which statement about some endangered species is accurate?","choices":["They can survive only in zoos.","They are harmed by field research.","They were never found in the wild.","They have been reintroduced to the wild from zoo-bred populations."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Several species \"have been reintroduced from zoo-bred populations.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.fe48f34b4b","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-331","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some technology writers predict that printed books will soon disappear, replaced entirely by e-books.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>E-book sales grew rapidly for several years after e-readers were introduced, but they later leveled off, and print books continue to account for a large share of sales. Surveys suggest that many readers value the physical experience of print, while others prefer e-books for travel. The two formats appear likely to coexist.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By arguing that e-books are unpopular with travelers","By agreeing that print books will disappear soon","By arguing that sales trends and reader preferences suggest print and e-books will continue to coexist","By claiming that e-book sales have never grown"],"answer":"C","explanation":"E-book growth leveled off, and print remains popular. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.76769559a8","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-332","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Many people believe that sugar makes children hyperactive, and parents often report that their children become wild after eating sweets.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>In controlled experiments, children were given either sugar or a sugar-free substitute without anyone knowing which. Researchers found no difference in behavior. In one study, parents who were told their child had eaten sugar rated the child as more hyperactive, even when the child had received the substitute, suggesting that expectations may shape what parents notice.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely say that the parents' reports described in Text 1","choices":["may reflect their expectations rather than an effect of sugar.","prove that sugar causes hyperactivity.","show that sugar calms children.","are based on controlled experiments."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Expectations may shape what parents notice. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.397a6d41c9","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-333","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some farmers argue that bees are the only pollinators that matter for crops.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Honeybees and wild bees are important, but studies of crop fields around the world show that flies, beetles, moths, and butterflies also pollinate many crops. In some fields, these insects account for a substantial share of flower visits. Protecting a variety of pollinators may make crop production more resilient if bee populations decline.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By agreeing that only bees pollinate crops","By claiming that bees are unimportant for crops","By acknowledging bees' importance while arguing that many other insects also contribute to pollination","By suggesting that beetles damage all crops"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Flies, beetles, moths, and butterflies also pollinate. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.5999d9c1c9","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-333","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some farmers argue that bees are the only pollinators that matter for crops.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Honeybees and wild bees are important, but studies of crop fields around the world show that flies, beetles, moths, and butterflies also pollinate many crops. In some fields, these insects account for a substantial share of flower visits. Protecting a variety of pollinators may make crop production more resilient if bee populations decline.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, protecting a variety of pollinators could be valuable because","choices":["other insects produce honey.","moths are easier to raise than bees.","bees would stop visiting flowers.","crops would depend less on bees alone if bee numbers fell."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Diversity makes production \"more resilient.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.a50f5d55b3","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-334","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some critics claim that remote learning is always less effective than in-person instruction.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Results from remote courses vary widely. Studies suggest that well-designed online courses with frequent interaction, clear structure, and regular feedback can produce learning outcomes similar to in-person courses. Poorly designed courses, and students without reliable internet or quiet study spaces, often fare worse.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By arguing that remote learning's effectiveness depends on course design and students' circumstances","By agreeing that remote learning is always less effective","By suggesting that feedback harms learning","By claiming that online courses are always more effective"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Outcomes vary by design and student conditions. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.6f55b56d01","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-334","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some critics claim that remote learning is always less effective than in-person instruction.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Results from remote courses vary widely. Studies suggest that well-designed online courses with frequent interaction, clear structure, and regular feedback can produce learning outcomes similar to in-person courses. Poorly designed courses, and students without reliable internet or quiet study spaces, often fare worse.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, which students are most likely to struggle in remote courses?","choices":["Students who receive regular feedback","Students without reliable internet or quiet places to study","Students who interact frequently with instructors","Students in well-structured courses"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Those lacking internet or quiet space \"often fare worse.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.c89616cbd5","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-334","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some critics claim that remote learning is always less effective than in-person instruction.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Results from remote courses vary widely. Studies suggest that well-designed online courses with frequent interaction, clear structure, and regular feedback can produce learning outcomes similar to in-person courses. Poorly designed courses, and students without reliable internet or quiet study spaces, often fare worse.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely object to the word \"always\" in Text 1 because","choices":["studies of remote learning do not exist.","in-person courses never work.","some remote courses produce outcomes similar to in-person courses.","all remote courses are ineffective."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Well-designed courses can match in-person results. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.d148109b41","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-335","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some historians have credited a single general's brilliant strategy for an army's victory in a famous battle.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Letters and supply records from the campaign reveal that the victorious army had far better access to food and ammunition than its opponent, whose supply lines had been disrupted by weeks of heavy rain. The general's decisions mattered, but historians who emphasize only strategy may overlook the practical conditions that made victory possible.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, the opposing army's supply problems were caused mainly by","choices":["a lack of ammunition factories.","the general's secret attack on its camp.","a shortage of soldiers.","weeks of heavy rain that disrupted its supply lines."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Supply lines \"had been disrupted by weeks of heavy rain.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.4eec56a3c6","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-335","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some historians have credited a single general's brilliant strategy for an army's victory in a famous battle.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Letters and supply records from the campaign reveal that the victorious army had far better access to food and ammunition than its opponent, whose supply lines had been disrupted by weeks of heavy rain. The general's decisions mattered, but historians who emphasize only strategy may overlook the practical conditions that made victory possible.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely agree with the historians in Text 1 that","choices":["supplies were irrelevant to the outcome.","the general lost the battle.","the general's decisions influenced the battle.","rain helped the victorious army's opponent."],"answer":"C","explanation":"\"The general's decisions mattered.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.ea8e60f704","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-336","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some public health officials argue that warning labels on cigarette packages are an effective way to reduce smoking.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Studies comparing countries suggest that large graphic warning images are associated with more attempts to quit than small text-only warnings. However, labels appear to work best as part of a broader strategy that includes taxes, smoking bans in public places, and support programs for people trying to quit.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By arguing that text-only warnings work better than images","By suggesting that taxes discourage people from quitting","By agreeing that labels, especially graphic ones, can help, while suggesting they are most effective combined with other measures","By claiming that warning labels increase smoking"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Graphic warnings help most within a broader strategy. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.1bd23627ed","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-336","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some public health officials argue that warning labels on cigarette packages are an effective way to reduce smoking.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Studies comparing countries suggest that large graphic warning images are associated with more attempts to quit than small text-only warnings. However, labels appear to work best as part of a broader strategy that includes taxes, smoking bans in public places, and support programs for people trying to quit.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, which type of warning label is associated with more attempts to quit?","choices":["Labels printed on the inside of packages","Large graphic images","Labels without any images or text","Small text-only warnings"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Graphic images are linked to more quit attempts. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.0c2dee837e","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-336","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some public health officials argue that warning labels on cigarette packages are an effective way to reduce smoking.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Studies comparing countries suggest that large graphic warning images are associated with more attempts to quit than small text-only warnings. However, labels appear to work best as part of a broader strategy that includes taxes, smoking bans in public places, and support programs for people trying to quit.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes how Text 2 relates to Text 1?","choices":["It discusses an unrelated health issue.","It restates the claim in Text 1 without evidence.","It rejects the claim in Text 1 entirely.","It supports the claim in Text 1 while adding an important qualification."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Labels help, but best as part of broader efforts. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.4a19ed1341","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-337","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Many runners believe that expensive, highly cushioned running shoes prevent injuries.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Studies of runners wearing different kinds of shoes have not consistently found that cushioning reduces injury rates. Some evidence suggests that choosing a shoe that feels comfortable to the individual runner may matter more than the amount of cushioning or the price.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 mentions price mainly to","choices":["recommend buying shoes on sale.","suggest that a shoe's cost is not a reliable guide to injury prevention.","explain why cushioned shoes are expensive.","argue that cheap shoes cause injuries."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Comfort may matter more \"than ... the price.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.dba43aa1da","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-340","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some people believe that reading on screens damages eyesight permanently.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Long periods of screen use can cause temporary eye strain, dryness, and blurred vision, often because people blink less while staring at screens. Eye doctors recommend regular breaks, such as looking at something distant every twenty minutes. There is little evidence, however, that screen use by itself causes permanent damage to adult vision.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By acknowledging that screens can cause temporary discomfort while arguing there is little evidence of permanent damage","By claiming that screens improve vision","By suggesting that people should stop blinking","By agreeing that screens permanently damage eyesight"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Temporary strain, but little evidence of lasting harm. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.4acad7078d","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-341","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some economists argue that automation will eliminate most jobs within a few decades.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Past waves of automation, such as the mechanization of farming, eliminated many specific jobs but also created new types of work, from factory employment to services. Automation can cause serious hardship for workers whose skills become obsolete, but history suggests that total employment has tended to recover as economies adapt, though often slowly and unevenly.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely agree with Text 1 that automation","choices":["can eliminate specific jobs.","creates no new work.","affects all workers equally.","will end all employment."],"answer":"A","explanation":"\"Eliminated many specific jobs.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.50c2a0af0b","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-342","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some coaches argue that young athletes should specialize in a single sport early to develop elite skills.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Studies of college and professional athletes found that many played several sports during childhood and specialized only in their teens. Early specialization has been linked to higher rates of overuse injuries and burnout, while playing multiple sports may develop a broader range of physical skills.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By agreeing that all young athletes should specialize early","By arguing that playing multiple sports causes injuries","By presenting evidence that many elite athletes played multiple sports early and that early specialization carries risks","By claiming that professional athletes never played other sports"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Many elite athletes diversified; specialization is linked to injuries and burnout. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.6731669d55","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-342","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some coaches argue that young athletes should specialize in a single sport early to develop elite skills.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Studies of college and professional athletes found that many played several sports during childhood and specialized only in their teens. Early specialization has been linked to higher rates of overuse injuries and burnout, while playing multiple sports may develop a broader range of physical skills.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, early specialization has been linked to","choices":["fewer injuries.","more overuse injuries and burnout.","greater enjoyment of sports.","a broader range of skills."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Linked to \"higher rates of overuse injuries and burnout.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.5c68a193a6","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-343","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some critics argue that translated poems are pale imitations that cannot capture the original work.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Translation inevitably changes a poem, since sounds, rhythms, and wordplay rarely survive intact in another language. Yet many translators see their work as creating a new poem that conveys the spirit of the original. Some translated poems have been admired as works of art in their own right and have introduced readers to writers they could never otherwise read.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By acknowledging that translation changes poems while arguing that translations can be valuable works of art in their own right","By claiming that translation preserves every sound and rhythm","By agreeing that translations have no value","By suggesting that readers should learn every language"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Changes are inevitable, but translations can be admired. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.bf0d74755c","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-343","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some critics argue that translated poems are pale imitations that cannot capture the original work.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Translation inevitably changes a poem, since sounds, rhythms, and wordplay rarely survive intact in another language. Yet many translators see their work as creating a new poem that conveys the spirit of the original. Some translated poems have been admired as works of art in their own right and have introduced readers to writers they could never otherwise read.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely agree with the critics in Text 1 that","choices":["translation cannot preserve every aspect of a poem.","wordplay translates perfectly.","translated poems are worthless.","poets should never be translated."],"answer":"A","explanation":"\"Sounds, rhythms, and wordplay rarely survive intact.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.6fc14631b6","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-343","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some critics argue that translated poems are pale imitations that cannot capture the original work.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Translation inevitably changes a poem, since sounds, rhythms, and wordplay rarely survive intact in another language. Yet many translators see their work as creating a new poem that conveys the spirit of the original. Some translated poems have been admired as works of art in their own right and have introduced readers to writers they could never otherwise read.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, one benefit of translated poems is that they","choices":["introduce readers to writers they could not otherwise read.","replace the original poems.","preserve every rhyme exactly.","make original languages unnecessary."],"answer":"A","explanation":"They \"introduced readers to writers they could never otherwise read.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.4cb11e3945","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-344","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some residents oppose building apartment complexes in their neighborhoods, arguing that new housing will lower nearby property values.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Studies of several cities found that new apartment buildings generally had little effect on nearby home values, and in some cases values rose slightly as new residents supported local shops and services. Effects varied, but the fear of widespread declines was not supported by most of the evidence.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, property values sometimes rose near new apartment buildings because","choices":["homeowners renovated their houses.","the apartments were demolished.","fewer people lived in the neighborhood.","new residents supported local shops and services."],"answer":"D","explanation":"New residents \"supported local shops and services.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.100f7067f7","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-346","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Many people think that the Great Wall of China was built all at once as a single continuous structure.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>The walls known collectively as the Great Wall were built, rebuilt, and extended by different dynasties over roughly two thousand years. Many sections are separate walls rather than one continuous line, and they were constructed from local materials ranging from rammed earth to stone and brick.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, sections of the Great Wall differ in their materials because","choices":["every section was designed by the same person.","builders used locally available materials.","the wall was built in a single year.","brick was the only material available."],"answer":"B","explanation":"\"Constructed from local materials.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.49a1699bdc","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-347","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some parents believe that children who watch educational television learn as much as children who read books.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Studies of young children suggest that well-designed educational programs can teach vocabulary and early math concepts. However, children tend to learn more when adults watch with them and discuss what they see, and shared book reading exposes children to a wider range of vocabulary than most television programs do.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, children learn more from educational programs when","choices":["they watch for many hours a day.","they watch alone.","programs have no vocabulary words.","adults watch with them and discuss what they see."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Learning improves with adult discussion. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.fa18b36544","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-348","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some scientists have suggested that humans can survive on Mars in the near future by building domed cities.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Mars presents major challenges beyond breathable air: its thin atmosphere provides little protection from radiation, temperatures swing drastically, and dust storms can last for weeks. Engineers are studying underground habitats and radiation shielding, but many researchers believe that long-term settlement will require technologies that do not yet exist.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, engineers are studying underground habitats most likely because they","choices":["are easier to see from Earth.","could offer protection from radiation.","would prevent all dust storms.","require no materials."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Radiation shielding is a key concern. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.40e5cae3e8","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-348","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some scientists have suggested that humans can survive on Mars in the near future by building domed cities.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Mars presents major challenges beyond breathable air: its thin atmosphere provides little protection from radiation, temperatures swing drastically, and dust storms can last for weeks. Engineers are studying underground habitats and radiation shielding, but many researchers believe that long-term settlement will require technologies that do not yet exist.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the relationship between the texts?","choices":["Text 2 supports Text 1 by listing completed Martian cities.","Text 2 discusses the Moon rather than Mars.","Text 2 describes obstacles that complicate the optimistic suggestion in Text 1.","Text 2 agrees that settlement is easy."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Challenges make near-term settlement difficult. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.ea14e80387","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-349","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some art critics dismiss street art as vandalism with no artistic merit.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Unauthorized graffiti can damage property, and many cities prohibit it. Yet some street artists create commissioned murals that neighborhoods welcome, and several museums have exhibited works by artists who began on the street. Whether a piece counts as vandalism or art may depend on permission, context, and community response.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By claiming that graffiti never damages property","By arguing that street art can have artistic merit and that its status depends on factors such as permission and context","By suggesting that museums should ban street artists","By agreeing that all street art is vandalism"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Commissioned murals and museum exhibitions show merit. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.8f37bd990c","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-350","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some educators argue that students learn best when teachers explain concepts directly before students practice.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Other educators favor having students attempt a problem before receiving instruction. Studies suggest that struggling with a problem first can help students notice what they do not understand, making the later explanation more meaningful. However, this approach works best when students have enough background knowledge to make progress; complete beginners may simply become frustrated.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, attempting a problem before instruction can be helpful because it","choices":["helps students recognize gaps in their understanding.","replaces the need for explanation.","works best for complete beginners.","makes students more frustrated."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Struggle helps students \"notice what they do not understand.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.0eef15afc9","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-350","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some educators argue that students learn best when teachers explain concepts directly before students practice.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Other educators favor having students attempt a problem before receiving instruction. Studies suggest that struggling with a problem first can help students notice what they do not understand, making the later explanation more meaningful. However, this approach works best when students have enough background knowledge to make progress; complete beginners may simply become frustrated.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely agree that the approach described in Text 1 may be especially suitable for","choices":["students who dislike explanations.","complete beginners with little background knowledge.","experts who have mastered a topic.","advanced students preparing for competitions."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Beginners \"may simply become frustrated\" by struggling first. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.0aab1ea1bd","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-352","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some nutrition writers claim that drinking eight glasses of water a day is necessary for good health.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>The body's water needs vary with climate, activity, and body size, and much of the water people need comes from food and other beverages. Most healthy adults can rely on thirst to guide how much they drink. The eight-glass rule may be a useful reminder for some people but is not a scientific requirement for everyone.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely say that the eight-glass rule","choices":["is a proven scientific requirement.","may help some people but is not required for everyone.","should be replaced by twenty glasses.","is harmful to everyone."],"answer":"B","explanation":"\"A useful reminder for some people.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.fffe189f1b","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-353","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some historians argue that the invention of the printing press immediately transformed European society.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Printing did make books cheaper and more widely available, but literacy rates rose slowly, and for decades most printed works were religious texts and classical writings already familiar to scholars. The press's broader social effects unfolded over generations as schooling expanded and new kinds of writing, such as newspapers, developed.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, one reason the printing press's social effects were gradual is that","choices":["newspapers were printed first.","scholars stopped reading.","literacy rates increased slowly.","books became more expensive."],"answer":"C","explanation":"\"Literacy rates rose slowly.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.e6c2645f32","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-354","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some psychologists argue that people make better decisions when they carefully analyze every option.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>For complex choices with many factors, such as choosing an apartment, some studies have found that people who thought carefully and then took a break before deciding made choices they were later more satisfied with. For simple decisions, careful analysis works well. Deliberation is valuable, but the best approach may depend on the type of decision.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By arguing that simple decisions require breaks","By agreeing that careful analysis is best for every decision","By suggesting that the best decision-making approach may depend on the complexity of the decision","By claiming that people should never think about decisions"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The approach depends on the decision type. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.32c2c02daa","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-355","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Many people believe that goldfish have memories lasting only a few seconds.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Experiments have shown that goldfish can be trained to respond to sounds or colors associated with feeding and can remember these associations for months. Some goldfish have learned to push levers for food at particular times of day. Their memory is far better than the popular belief suggests.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 mentions goldfish pushing levers mainly to","choices":["explain how aquariums are cleaned.","provide an additional example of goldfish learning.","show that goldfish are aggressive.","argue that goldfish should not be fed."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It shows learning tied to times of day. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.13b27d6584","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-356","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some writers claim that people learn best when information matches their preferred \"learning style,\" such as visual or auditory.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Studies that tested students' preferred styles and then taught them in matching or mismatching ways found little difference in learning. Students may prefer certain formats, but most people learn more from combining words, images, and active practice than from a single mode. Researchers suggest that the content being taught, not an individual's supposed style, should guide instruction.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, researchers recommend that instruction be guided mainly by","choices":["the number of students in the class.","each student's preferred learning style.","the nature of the content being taught.","the teacher's favorite format."],"answer":"C","explanation":"\"The content being taught ... should guide instruction.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.0525ffc56c","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-357","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some city officials argue that increasing police patrols is the most effective way to reduce crime in neighborhoods.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Research in several cities has found that improving street lighting and cleaning up vacant lots were associated with reductions in certain crimes, sometimes at lower cost than additional patrols. Researchers suggest that changes to the physical environment can complement other public safety strategies.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By arguing that vacant lots should be left untouched","By claiming that street lighting increases crime","By agreeing that patrols are the only effective approach","By presenting evidence that environmental improvements can also reduce crime and may complement other strategies"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Lighting and lot cleanup reduced some crimes. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.a983af3d52","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-357","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some city officials argue that increasing police patrols is the most effective way to reduce crime in neighborhoods.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Research in several cities has found that improving street lighting and cleaning up vacant lots were associated with reductions in certain crimes, sometimes at lower cost than additional patrols. Researchers suggest that changes to the physical environment can complement other public safety strategies.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes how Text 2 relates to Text 1?","choices":["It discusses crime in a different century.","It provides evidence that patrols increase crime.","It suggests additional approaches beyond the one proposed in Text 1.","It agrees that patrols are the most effective approach."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Environmental changes \"complement other public safety strategies.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.136ca57dd7","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-358","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some biologists have argued that animals that live in groups always benefit from doing so, because groups offer protection from predators.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Group living can reduce predation, but it also has costs. Animals in large groups must compete for food, and diseases spread more easily among closely packed individuals. Whether group living is beneficial overall depends on factors such as food availability and predation pressure.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, one cost of living in a large group is that","choices":["animals cannot communicate.","diseases can spread more easily.","animals find food more easily.","predators become more common."],"answer":"B","explanation":"\"Diseases spread more easily among closely packed individuals.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.66c7279b13","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-359","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some critics argue that audiences prefer films based on original stories rather than sequels and remakes.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Box office data from recent decades show that sequels and remakes have frequently been among the highest-earning films each year. Surveys, however, suggest that many viewers say they want more original films, even as they buy tickets to familiar franchises. What audiences say and what they choose may differ.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, the surveys and box office data together suggest that","choices":["viewers' stated preferences may not match their choices.","original films earn the most money.","viewers never answer surveys honestly.","audiences dislike all films."],"answer":"A","explanation":"\"What audiences say and what they choose may differ.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.eb3692c35a","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-360","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some ecologists claim that cats kept indoors live healthier lives than cats allowed outdoors.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Surveys of veterinarians and pet insurance records suggest that indoor cats face lower risks of injury from traffic, fights, and certain infectious diseases, and often live longer. However, some indoor cats become overweight or bored without enough activity, so veterinarians recommend providing play and climbing spaces.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By claiming that outdoor cats always live longer","By suggesting that cats should never be kept as pets","By supporting the claim while noting that indoor cats need enrichment to avoid problems such as weight gain and boredom","By arguing that indoor cats face more injuries from traffic"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Lower risks indoors, with a caveat about activity. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.03f5eb77f5","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-360","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some ecologists claim that cats kept indoors live healthier lives than cats allowed outdoors.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Surveys of veterinarians and pet insurance records suggest that indoor cats face lower risks of injury from traffic, fights, and certain infectious diseases, and often live longer. However, some indoor cats become overweight or bored without enough activity, so veterinarians recommend providing play and climbing spaces.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the relationship between the texts?","choices":["Text 2 discusses dogs rather than cats.","Text 2 restates Text 1 without evidence.","Text 2 supports Text 1's claim but adds a qualification.","Text 2 rejects Text 1's claim entirely."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Supportive evidence plus the need for enrichment. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.1a3d280932","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-361","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some historians describe a famous explorer as the first person to reach a particular region of North America.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Indigenous peoples had lived in the region for thousands of years before the explorer arrived, and oral histories and archaeological sites document their long presence. The explorer's journey was significant for European knowledge of the area, but describing him as the first to reach it overlooks the people who already lived there.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 acknowledges that the explorer's journey","choices":["was the first human arrival in the region.","was significant for European knowledge of the region.","was invented by historians.","had no historical importance."],"answer":"B","explanation":"\"Significant for European knowledge of the area.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.9822741178","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-362","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some scientists argue that planting trees is one of the most effective ways to fight climate change.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Trees absorb carbon dioxide as they grow, and restoring forests can store significant amounts of carbon. However, new forests take decades to mature, planting in the wrong places can harm grasslands or water supplies, and trees can release stored carbon if they burn. Many researchers see tree planting as helpful but not a substitute for reducing emissions.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By agreeing that tree planting alone will solve climate change","By arguing that forests should be cut down","By claiming that trees do not absorb carbon dioxide","By agreeing that trees store carbon while cautioning about limitations and arguing that planting cannot replace emission reductions"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Benefits plus limits; not a substitute. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.e00a56cbbf","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-362","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some scientists argue that planting trees is one of the most effective ways to fight climate change.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Trees absorb carbon dioxide as they grow, and restoring forests can store significant amounts of carbon. However, new forests take decades to mature, planting in the wrong places can harm grasslands or water supplies, and trees can release stored carbon if they burn. Many researchers see tree planting as helpful but not a substitute for reducing emissions.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, one risk of planting trees in the wrong places is that it can","choices":["prevent all forest fires.","harm grasslands or water supplies.","make trees grow faster.","reduce emissions from cars."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Planting in the wrong places \"can harm grasslands or water supplies.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.047bbff5d7","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-362","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some scientists argue that planting trees is one of the most effective ways to fight climate change.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Trees absorb carbon dioxide as they grow, and restoring forests can store significant amounts of carbon. However, new forests take decades to mature, planting in the wrong places can harm grasslands or water supplies, and trees can release stored carbon if they burn. Many researchers see tree planting as helpful but not a substitute for reducing emissions.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely agree with the scientists in Text 1 that","choices":["restoring forests can store significant amounts of carbon.","tree planting is a complete solution.","emissions do not need to be reduced.","forests never burn."],"answer":"A","explanation":"\"Restoring forests can store significant amounts of carbon.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.a055f0e3b5","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-363","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Many people believe that humans use only ten percent of their brains.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Brain imaging shows activity throughout the brain, though not all regions are equally active at the same moment. Damage to almost any area of the brain can affect abilities, which would be unlikely if most of the brain went unused. Neuroscientists regard the ten-percent idea as a myth.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By claiming that brain imaging shows no activity","By agreeing that most of the brain is unused","By citing brain imaging and effects of brain damage as evidence that people use far more than ten percent of their brains","By arguing that brain damage has no effects"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Activity throughout the brain and effects of damage. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.4736a88b56","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-364","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some urban planners argue that tall residential towers are the best way to house growing city populations.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Towers can house many people on small plots of land, but mid-rise buildings of four to eight stories can achieve similar densities across a neighborhood while keeping residents closer to street life. Some studies suggest that residents of mid-rise neighborhoods report stronger connections with neighbors than residents of isolated high-rise towers.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By agreeing that towers are the only way to increase density","By arguing that cities should stop growing","By claiming that mid-rise buildings house fewer people than single-family homes","By suggesting that mid-rise buildings can provide similar density with social advantages"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Mid-rises achieve density while supporting connections. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.2f8d99632c","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-364","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some urban planners argue that tall residential towers are the best way to house growing city populations.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Towers can house many people on small plots of land, but mid-rise buildings of four to eight stories can achieve similar densities across a neighborhood while keeping residents closer to street life. Some studies suggest that residents of mid-rise neighborhoods report stronger connections with neighbors than residents of isolated high-rise towers.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, an advantage of mid-rise neighborhoods is that residents","choices":["require more land per person than towers in every case.","may feel more connected to their neighbors.","have fewer neighbors.","live farther from street life."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Residents \"report stronger connections.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.bb69e9ef9f","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-365","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some educators argue that grades motivate students to work hard.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Grades can encourage effort, especially for students focused on college admissions. But research suggests that when grades become the main goal, some students choose easier tasks to protect their scores and lose interest in learning for its own sake. Feedback that explains how to improve may sustain motivation more effectively for many students.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, when grades become students' main goal, some students","choices":["choose easier tasks to protect their scores.","stop attending school.","seek out the hardest tasks.","ignore their grades."],"answer":"A","explanation":"\"Some students choose easier tasks to protect their scores.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.326b7239a8","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-366","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some historians claim that medieval people believed the Earth was flat.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Educated writers throughout the Middle Ages generally accepted that the Earth was round, a view inherited from ancient Greek scholars. Textbooks used in medieval universities described a spherical Earth. The idea that medieval people commonly believed in a flat Earth became popular much later, partly through nineteenth-century writers.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By agreeing that medieval people believed the Earth was flat","By arguing that universities did not exist in the Middle Ages","By arguing that educated medieval people generally knew the Earth was round and that the flat-Earth belief is a later myth","By claiming that ancient Greeks believed the Earth was flat"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Medieval textbooks described a spherical Earth. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.591b82bbc2","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-366","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some historians claim that medieval people believed the Earth was flat.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Educated writers throughout the Middle Ages generally accepted that the Earth was round, a view inherited from ancient Greek scholars. Textbooks used in medieval universities described a spherical Earth. The idea that medieval people commonly believed in a flat Earth became popular much later, partly through nineteenth-century writers.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, the idea that medieval people believed in a flat Earth","choices":["was accepted by all medieval scholars.","was proven by ancient Greeks.","became popular much later, partly through nineteenth-century writers.","came from medieval university textbooks."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It \"became popular much later.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.a348e9b9f2","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-367","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some people argue that working longer hours always leads to greater productivity.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Studies of factory and office workers have found that output per hour tends to decline when work weeks become very long. Beyond a certain point, fatigue leads to more errors and accidents, so additional hours add little or even reduce total output. Many companies have found that moderate schedules with adequate rest sustain productivity better.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, very long work weeks can reduce output mainly because","choices":["workers have too much rest.","companies pay workers less.","factories close earlier.","fatigue causes more errors and accidents."],"answer":"D","explanation":"\"Fatigue leads to more errors and accidents.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.68c3f33118","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-367","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some people argue that working longer hours always leads to greater productivity.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Studies of factory and office workers have found that output per hour tends to decline when work weeks become very long. Beyond a certain point, fatigue leads to more errors and accidents, so additional hours add little or even reduce total output. Many companies have found that moderate schedules with adequate rest sustain productivity better.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely object to the word \"always\" in Text 1 because","choices":["productivity cannot be measured.","additional hours eventually stop increasing productivity.","workers never work long hours.","rest always reduces output."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Beyond a point, extra hours add little. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.84f40f99fe","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-368","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some critics argue that video calls can fully replace in-person meetings for business teams.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Video calls save travel time and allow teams in different cities to meet easily. However, surveys of workers report that brainstorming and building trust with new colleagues often feel more difficult over video, and informal conversations before and after meetings are less common. Many teams now mix video calls with occasional in-person gatherings.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By claiming that video calls waste time","By acknowledging the benefits of video calls while arguing that some kinds of collaboration work better in person","By arguing that teams should never meet in person","By agreeing that video calls fully replace in-person meetings"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Brainstorming and trust-building can suffer over video. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.5f01c8eb33","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-369","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some nutrition writers argue that fresh vegetables are always more nutritious than frozen ones.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Vegetables are often frozen within hours of harvest, preserving many vitamins, while fresh vegetables may spend days in transport and storage, losing nutrients along the way. Studies comparing the two have found that frozen vegetables are often similar in nutrients to fresh ones and sometimes contain more of certain vitamins.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, fresh vegetables may lose nutrients because they","choices":["are grown in greenhouses.","spend days in transport and storage.","contain added preservatives.","are frozen too quickly."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Transport and storage cause losses. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.1a7341d515","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-370","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some writers say that the Industrial Revolution made life worse for ordinary workers.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Early factory work was often dangerous, hours were long, and crowded cities bred disease. Over time, however, rising productivity contributed to higher wages, cheaper goods, and eventually to reforms such as limits on child labor and working hours. The period's effects on workers changed considerably over the course of the century.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By acknowledging early hardships while arguing that conditions for workers improved over time","By claiming that factory work was always safe","By arguing that cities had no disease","By agreeing that life only worsened throughout the period"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Early hardship, later improvements and reforms. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.9115137db8","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-370","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some writers say that the Industrial Revolution made life worse for ordinary workers.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Early factory work was often dangerous, hours were long, and crowded cities bred disease. Over time, however, rising productivity contributed to higher wages, cheaper goods, and eventually to reforms such as limits on child labor and working hours. The period's effects on workers changed considerably over the course of the century.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely agree with the writers in Text 1 that","choices":["cities were free of disease.","workers' lives never improved.","early factory work was often dangerous.","reforms made conditions worse."],"answer":"C","explanation":"\"Early factory work was often dangerous.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.72b66931c7","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-371","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some scientists have claimed that birds' feathers first evolved to help them fly.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Fossils show that several dinosaurs that could not fly already had feathers. Some of these feathers were simple filaments that likely provided insulation, and others may have been used in displays to attract mates. Feathers may have been adapted for flight only later in the history of birds' ancestors.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, simple filament feathers in some dinosaurs likely helped with","choices":["swimming.","insulation.","digging burrows.","gliding long distances."],"answer":"B","explanation":"They \"likely provided insulation.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.5fb4752f17","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-371","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some scientists have claimed that birds' feathers first evolved to help them fly.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Fossils show that several dinosaurs that could not fly already had feathers. Some of these feathers were simple filaments that likely provided insulation, and others may have been used in displays to attract mates. Feathers may have been adapted for flight only later in the history of birds' ancestors.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the relationship between the texts?","choices":["Text 2 agrees that dinosaurs could fly.","Text 2 discusses the flight of insects.","Text 2 confirms that feathers evolved for flight.","Text 2 offers evidence suggesting a different origin for feathers than the one proposed in Text 1."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Feathers predate flight. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.e5d49e4788","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-372","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some parents worry that children who have imaginary friends are lonely or have trouble making real friends.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Studies of preschool children have found that those with imaginary companions are generally as socially skilled as other children, and some show especially strong abilities to understand other people's perspectives. Imaginary friends appear to be a common and healthy part of development for many children.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By claiming that imaginary friends harm development","By agreeing that imaginary friends indicate loneliness","By presenting evidence that children with imaginary friends are typically socially skilled","By arguing that preschool children cannot make friends"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Studies show normal or strong social skills. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.8fc8df4ff6","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-372","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some parents worry that children who have imaginary friends are lonely or have trouble making real friends.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Studies of preschool children have found that those with imaginary companions are generally as socially skilled as other children, and some show especially strong abilities to understand other people's perspectives. Imaginary friends appear to be a common and healthy part of development for many children.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, some children with imaginary companions show particular strength in","choices":["memorizing numbers.","understanding other people's perspectives.","physical coordination.","spelling."],"answer":"B","explanation":"\"Especially strong abilities to understand other people's perspectives.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.6e6e9931cb","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-372","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some parents worry that children who have imaginary friends are lonely or have trouble making real friends.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Studies of preschool children have found that those with imaginary companions are generally as socially skilled as other children, and some show especially strong abilities to understand other people's perspectives. Imaginary friends appear to be a common and healthy part of development for many children.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely describe imaginary friends as","choices":["rare among preschool children.","harmful to friendships.","common and healthy for many children.","a sign of serious problems."],"answer":"C","explanation":"\"A common and healthy part of development.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.406c798bc3","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-373","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some energy experts argue that nuclear power is too dangerous to be part of efforts to reduce carbon emissions.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Nuclear accidents have caused serious harm, and long-term storage of radioactive waste remains a challenge. Yet measured by deaths per unit of electricity produced, nuclear power has a safety record comparable to wind and solar and far better than coal. Some researchers argue that its risks should be weighed against the harms of fossil fuels.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely agree with the experts in Text 1 that","choices":["nuclear power is more dangerous than coal.","nuclear accidents have caused serious harm.","waste storage is easy.","wind power is unsafe."],"answer":"B","explanation":"\"Nuclear accidents have caused serious harm.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.bde8e017f8","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-374","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some writers argue that handwriting is no longer an important skill in the digital age.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Adults increasingly type rather than write by hand, but studies of young children suggest that learning to form letters by hand helps them recognize letters and learn to read. Handwritten notes may also help some students remember information better than typed notes. The value of handwriting may lie partly in its effects on learning, not only in its everyday use.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By arguing that handwriting may benefit learning even if adults rarely use it","By arguing that children should never type","By claiming that adults write more by hand than they type","By agreeing that handwriting has no value"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Handwriting helps letter recognition and memory. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.762960cfea","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-374","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some writers argue that handwriting is no longer an important skill in the digital age.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Adults increasingly type rather than write by hand, but studies of young children suggest that learning to form letters by hand helps them recognize letters and learn to read. Handwritten notes may also help some students remember information better than typed notes. The value of handwriting may lie partly in its effects on learning, not only in its everyday use.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, learning to form letters by hand may help young children","choices":["recognize letters and learn to read.","type faster.","draw pictures more accurately.","avoid using computers."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It \"helps them recognize letters and learn to read.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.e6d0fc81e3","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-375","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some sports commentators claim that home teams win more often mainly because of cheering crowds.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>When sports leagues played games without fans during a period of restrictions, the home advantage shrank in some sports but did not disappear. Researchers suggest that familiarity with the venue, reduced travel fatigue, and referees' decisions all contribute, with crowds being one factor among several.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By claiming that home teams never have an advantage","By arguing that referees always favor visiting teams","By agreeing that crowds are the only reason home teams win","By presenting evidence that crowds are only one of several factors behind home advantage"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Home advantage persisted without fans. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.35d820cfab","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-375","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some sports commentators claim that home teams win more often mainly because of cheering crowds.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>When sports leagues played games without fans during a period of restrictions, the home advantage shrank in some sports but did not disappear. Researchers suggest that familiarity with the venue, reduced travel fatigue, and referees' decisions all contribute, with crowds being one factor among several.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely agree with Text 1 that crowds","choices":["cause visiting teams to win.","may contribute to home advantage.","have no effect on games.","are the sole cause of home advantage."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Crowds are \"one factor among several.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.2b75cb932e","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-376","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some commentators argue that social media has made teenagers less happy.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Large surveys find a small average association between heavy social media use and lower well-being among teenagers, but the effect varies widely. For some teens, especially those who use it passively for many hours, social media is linked to worse mood; others use it to maintain friendships and find support. Researchers caution that the average link is modest and does not show that social media causes unhappiness.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By agreeing that social media makes all teenagers unhappy","By noting that the association is small, varies among teens, and does not establish causation","By claiming that social media always improves well-being","By arguing that teenagers do not use social media"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Modest, variable association without causal proof. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.3cd27fb1c8","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-376","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some commentators argue that social media has made teenagers less happy.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Large surveys find a small average association between heavy social media use and lower well-being among teenagers, but the effect varies widely. For some teens, especially those who use it passively for many hours, social media is linked to worse mood; others use it to maintain friendships and find support. Researchers caution that the average link is modest and does not show that social media causes unhappiness.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely say that the claim in Text 1","choices":["underestimates social media's harms.","overstates what the evidence shows.","ignores teenagers entirely.","is fully proven by surveys."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The link is modest and not causal. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.eda2e8d74a","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-377","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some farmers believe that plowing fields thoroughly before planting always produces the best harvests.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Plowing can control weeds and loosen soil, but it also exposes soil to erosion and can reduce the organic matter that holds moisture. Many farmers who have switched to no-till methods, planting directly into the remains of the previous crop, report healthier soil and similar yields over time, though weeds can be harder to manage.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By agreeing that plowing always produces the best harvests","By claiming that plowing has no benefits","By acknowledging plowing's benefits while noting its drawbacks and describing an alternative with comparable yields","By arguing that no-till methods eliminate weeds"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Plowing has pros and cons; no-till can match yields. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.09d63efbf0","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-377","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some farmers believe that plowing fields thoroughly before planting always produces the best harvests.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Plowing can control weeds and loosen soil, but it also exposes soil to erosion and can reduce the organic matter that holds moisture. Many farmers who have switched to no-till methods, planting directly into the remains of the previous crop, report healthier soil and similar yields over time, though weeds can be harder to manage.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, a challenge of no-till farming is that","choices":["yields always fall.","crops cannot be planted.","soil erodes more quickly.","weeds can be harder to manage."],"answer":"D","explanation":"\"Weeds can be harder to manage.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.973a6cd5a7","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-377","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some farmers believe that plowing fields thoroughly before planting always produces the best harvests.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Plowing can control weeds and loosen soil, but it also exposes soil to erosion and can reduce the organic matter that holds moisture. Many farmers who have switched to no-till methods, planting directly into the remains of the previous crop, report healthier soil and similar yields over time, though weeds can be harder to manage.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely agree with the farmers in Text 1 that plowing","choices":["prevents all erosion.","is the only way to plant crops.","always increases organic matter.","can help control weeds."],"answer":"D","explanation":"\"Plowing can control weeds and loosen soil.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.e25b40075b","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-378","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some art historians argue that a famous painter worked alone and developed his distinctive style entirely by himself.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Workshop records show that the painter employed several assistants who prepared canvases, mixed pigments, and sometimes painted backgrounds. Letters also reveal that he studied and admired the works of earlier artists. His style was distinctive, but it developed within a network of collaborators and influences.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely agree with the art historians in Text 1 that the painter's style","choices":["did not exist.","was copied from his assistants.","was identical to earlier artists' styles.","was distinctive."],"answer":"D","explanation":"\"His style was distinctive.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.26a3d0c9fe","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-378","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some art historians argue that a famous painter worked alone and developed his distinctive style entirely by himself.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Workshop records show that the painter employed several assistants who prepared canvases, mixed pigments, and sometimes painted backgrounds. Letters also reveal that he studied and admired the works of earlier artists. His style was distinctive, but it developed within a network of collaborators and influences.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, the painter's assistants","choices":["signed his paintings.","never touched the canvases.","sometimes painted backgrounds.","taught him to paint."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Assistants \"sometimes painted backgrounds.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.ffbde21d92","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-380","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some travelers believe that visiting popular tourist destinations during peak season offers the best experience.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Peak season often brings pleasant weather and full schedules of events. Yet crowds, long lines, and higher prices can make visits stressful. Many travelers who visit during the \"shoulder seasons\" just before or after peak times report mild weather, lower costs, and a more relaxed experience.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, the shoulder seasons are","choices":["periods just before or after peak times.","times when attractions are closed.","the busiest times for tourism.","the coldest months of the year."],"answer":"A","explanation":"\"Just before or after peak times.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.f7a88189f2","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-380","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some travelers believe that visiting popular tourist destinations during peak season offers the best experience.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Peak season often brings pleasant weather and full schedules of events. Yet crowds, long lines, and higher prices can make visits stressful. Many travelers who visit during the \"shoulder seasons\" just before or after peak times report mild weather, lower costs, and a more relaxed experience.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely agree with the travelers in Text 1 that peak season","choices":["is the least crowded time.","has the shortest lines.","often offers pleasant weather and many events.","is cheaper than other seasons."],"answer":"C","explanation":"\"Pleasant weather and full schedules of events.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.5e7bfb5f96","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-381","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some scientists claim that honeybees are the most important pollinators for wild plants.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Honeybees pollinate many crops, but studies of wild plant communities have found that native bees, such as bumblebees and solitary bees, often do more of the pollination. In some habitats, large honeybee colonies may even compete with native bees for flowers.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By arguing that native bees do not visit flowers","By claiming that honeybees pollinate no crops","By agreeing that honeybees pollinate most wild plants","By arguing that native bees often pollinate wild plants more than honeybees and that honeybees can compete with them"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Native bees do more wild pollination. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.d9f470f949","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-381","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some scientists claim that honeybees are the most important pollinators for wild plants.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Honeybees pollinate many crops, but studies of wild plant communities have found that native bees, such as bumblebees and solitary bees, often do more of the pollination. In some habitats, large honeybee colonies may even compete with native bees for flowers.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely agree with Text 1 that honeybees","choices":["are the only pollinators of wild plants.","cannot pollinate crops.","never compete with other bees.","pollinate many crops."],"answer":"D","explanation":"\"Honeybees pollinate many crops.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.a0ecf8c3b0","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-382","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some historians argue that a certain ancient empire fell primarily because of economic problems.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>The empire did suffer from inflation and heavy taxation, but it also faced repeated invasions, civil wars, and outbreaks of disease. Historians increasingly describe its decline as the result of many interacting pressures rather than a single cause.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely agree with the historians in Text 1 that the empire","choices":["experienced economic difficulties.","had no inflation.","fell because of disease alone.","never faced invasions."],"answer":"A","explanation":"\"The empire did suffer from inflation and heavy taxation.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.960422e176","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-383","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some writers argue that owning a pet improves people's health.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Some studies have linked dog ownership with more physical activity, since dog owners take regular walks. Other research finds that pet owners differ from non-owners in income, age, and lifestyle, which could explain some health differences. Pets may offer benefits, but the evidence that pets themselves improve health is mixed.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 mentions differences in income, age, and lifestyle mainly to","choices":["suggest other possible explanations for health differences.","show that pets are expensive.","describe who adopts pets.","argue that pets should be banned."],"answer":"A","explanation":"These \"could explain some health differences.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.13f5a769b9","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-384","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some economists argue that raising the minimum wage always leads to large job losses.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Studies comparing neighboring regions with different minimum wage increases have often found small or no effects on employment for moderate increases. Researchers caution, however, that very large increases, or increases in areas with low average wages, might have different effects that are harder to predict.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely object to the word \"always\" in Text 1 because","choices":["job losses cannot be measured.","all increases create jobs.","moderate increases have often shown little effect on employment.","minimum wages never change."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Many studies found small or no effects. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.8ac0664535","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-385","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some teachers believe that students should not use calculators until they have mastered arithmetic by hand.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Research suggests that calculators, used thoughtfully, do not harm basic skills and can let students focus on problem solving and patterns. However, studies also show that students benefit from understanding arithmetic concepts and developing number sense before relying heavily on calculators.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By claiming that calculators always harm basic skills","By arguing that students should never learn arithmetic","By agreeing that calculators should be banned entirely","By suggesting that calculators can be helpful when used thoughtfully, while agreeing that understanding arithmetic remains important"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Thoughtful use helps, but number sense matters. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.48d7f47f75","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-385","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some teachers believe that students should not use calculators until they have mastered arithmetic by hand.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Research suggests that calculators, used thoughtfully, do not harm basic skills and can let students focus on problem solving and patterns. However, studies also show that students benefit from understanding arithmetic concepts and developing number sense before relying heavily on calculators.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely agree with the teachers in Text 1 that","choices":["number sense cannot be taught.","problem solving is unimportant.","students benefit from understanding arithmetic concepts.","calculators should never be used."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Students \"benefit from understanding arithmetic concepts.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.8a3b63e386","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-386","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some scientists argue that whales are too large to be affected by small plastic particles in the ocean.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Filter-feeding whales swallow enormous volumes of water to catch tiny prey, and researchers analyzing their feeding have estimated that some whales may ingest millions of microplastic particles daily. The long-term health effects are still being studied, but the scale of exposure suggests that size does not protect whales from plastic pollution.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 acknowledges that","choices":["whales eat only large fish.","the long-term health effects are still being studied.","plastic pollution has ended.","microplastics are proven to kill all whales."],"answer":"B","explanation":"\"The long-term health effects are still being studied.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.e64026d9d2","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-387","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some writers claim that people who multitask are more productive.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Experiments show that switching between tasks takes time and increases errors, because the brain must reorient each time attention shifts. People who frequently multitask often perform worse on tests of attention than people who multitask less, although they tend to believe they are skilled at it.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By arguing that attention cannot be tested","By claiming that switching tasks takes no time","By presenting evidence that multitasking tends to reduce efficiency and increase errors","By agreeing that multitaskers are more productive"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Switching costs time and accuracy. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.e2370d01ec","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-388","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some urban officials argue that banning cars from city centers will harm local businesses.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>When several European cities closed central districts to most car traffic, some shops initially reported fewer customers. Within a few years, however, foot traffic increased in many of these districts, and surveys found that pedestrians often spent more time and money in shops than drivers had. Results varied by city and depended on transit access.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, the success of car-free districts depended partly on","choices":["the number of parking lots.","the color of shop signs.","access to public transit.","banning pedestrians."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Results \"depended on transit access.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.671f9773b0","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-388","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some urban officials argue that banning cars from city centers will harm local businesses.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>When several European cities closed central districts to most car traffic, some shops initially reported fewer customers. Within a few years, however, foot traffic increased in many of these districts, and surveys found that pedestrians often spent more time and money in shops than drivers had. Results varied by city and depended on transit access.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely agree with the officials in Text 1 that","choices":["all businesses close after car bans.","some shops may lose customers at first.","car bans have no effects.","pedestrians never shop."],"answer":"B","explanation":"\"Some shops initially reported fewer customers.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.7cc3022de1","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-389","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some literary critics argue that a novel's meaning is determined entirely by what its author intended.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Authors' intentions can illuminate their works, and letters or interviews often reveal what writers hoped to achieve. Yet readers from different times and places frequently find meanings the author never anticipated, and many critics argue that these interpretations can be valid if they are supported by the text itself.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By arguing that novels have no meaning","By agreeing that only the author's intention matters","By claiming that authors' intentions are always irrelevant","By acknowledging the value of authors' intentions while arguing that readers can find valid meanings beyond them"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Intentions help, but textual evidence supports other readings. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.793412d164","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-389","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some literary critics argue that a novel's meaning is determined entirely by what its author intended.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Authors' intentions can illuminate their works, and letters or interviews often reveal what writers hoped to achieve. Yet readers from different times and places frequently find meanings the author never anticipated, and many critics argue that these interpretations can be valid if they are supported by the text itself.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, a reader's interpretation is valid if it is","choices":["approved by the author's family.","the same as the author's intention.","published in a newspaper.","supported by the text itself."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Valid \"if they are supported by the text itself.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.a0e76b57a7","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-389","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some literary critics argue that a novel's meaning is determined entirely by what its author intended.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Authors' intentions can illuminate their works, and letters or interviews often reveal what writers hoped to achieve. Yet readers from different times and places frequently find meanings the author never anticipated, and many critics argue that these interpretations can be valid if they are supported by the text itself.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely agree with the critics in Text 1 that","choices":["readers' interpretations are never valid.","novels have one fixed meaning.","authors' intentions can shed light on their works.","letters from authors are unreliable."],"answer":"C","explanation":"\"Authors' intentions can illuminate their works.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.5a9a0ca343","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-390","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some researchers argue that fish do not feel pain because they lack certain brain structures found in mammals.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Fish have nerve receptors that respond to harmful stimuli, and experiments show that fish exposed to such stimuli change their behavior, avoiding areas where they were harmed and responding to pain-relieving drugs. Although fish brains differ from mammal brains, some scientists argue that these findings suggest fish may experience something like pain.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, evidence that fish may feel pain includes that fish","choices":["respond to pain-relieving drugs.","have the same brain structures as mammals.","lack nerve receptors.","never avoid harmful areas."],"answer":"A","explanation":"They respond \"to pain-relieving drugs.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.81d8cc91d0","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-391","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some parents believe that children should be praised for being smart to build their confidence.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Studies have found that children praised for intelligence were more likely to avoid challenging tasks afterward, perhaps fearing they would look less smart if they failed. Children praised for effort were more willing to try harder problems and persisted longer after mistakes.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, children praised for intelligence may avoid challenges because they","choices":["find easy tasks boring.","prefer working in groups.","do not understand praise.","fear failing would make them seem less smart."],"answer":"D","explanation":"\"Fearing they would look less smart.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.9247d1ddcd","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-392","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some geologists argue that earthquakes can be predicted precisely by monitoring animal behavior.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>There are many anecdotes of animals behaving strangely before earthquakes, but systematic studies have not found reliable patterns. Animals behave unusually for many reasons, and reports tend to be remembered after an earthquake occurs. Scientists instead focus on estimating the long-term probability of earthquakes in particular regions.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By claiming that animals never behave unusually","By arguing that earthquake risks cannot be estimated","By agreeing that animal behavior predicts earthquakes precisely","By arguing that systematic studies have not found reliable animal warning signs and that reports may be shaped by hindsight"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Anecdotes are unreliable and remembered afterward. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.5167bac1ee","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-392","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some geologists argue that earthquakes can be predicted precisely by monitoring animal behavior.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>There are many anecdotes of animals behaving strangely before earthquakes, but systematic studies have not found reliable patterns. Animals behave unusually for many reasons, and reports tend to be remembered after an earthquake occurs. Scientists instead focus on estimating the long-term probability of earthquakes in particular regions.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, scientists currently focus on","choices":["predicting the exact day of earthquakes.","training animals to detect earthquakes.","preventing earthquakes.","estimating the long-term probability of earthquakes in regions."],"answer":"D","explanation":"They estimate \"long-term probability.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.3e2e2be951","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-393","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some urban planners argue that suburbs are always worse for the environment than cities.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>City residents generally drive less and live in smaller homes, which tends to reduce energy use per person. However, some suburbs with good transit and compact development have lower emissions than car-dependent areas within city limits. The environmental impact depends more on density, transit, and housing type than on whether an area is labeled urban or suburban.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, a suburb could have lower emissions than part of a city if it","choices":["depends entirely on cars.","has larger homes.","has good transit and compact development.","has no public transportation."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Compact suburbs with transit can outperform car-dependent city areas. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.578463ad22","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-394","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some writers claim that the ancient city of Troy described in epic poetry was purely imaginary.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Excavations at a site in western Turkey have uncovered a city with multiple layers of settlement, including one destroyed around the time associated with the legendary war. While the poems contain myth and exaggeration, many archaeologists believe they may preserve memories of a real city and conflicts in the region.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By claiming that the poems are completely accurate historical records","By agreeing that Troy was entirely imaginary","By presenting archaeological evidence of a real city that may have inspired the poems, while acknowledging their mythical elements","By arguing that no cities existed in western Turkey"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Excavations suggest a real basis. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.4048dcc33c","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-395","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some health writers argue that vitamin supplements are necessary for everyone.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>People with certain conditions, diets, or life stages may benefit from specific supplements; for example, pregnant women are often advised to take folic acid. For most healthy adults who eat a varied diet, however, large studies have found little benefit from routine multivitamins in preventing major diseases.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By arguing that pregnant women should avoid folic acid","By claiming that supplements harm everyone","By arguing that supplements help some groups but offer little benefit for most healthy adults with varied diets","By agreeing that everyone needs supplements"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Targeted benefits, little general benefit. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."}
]);
